import ImperativeOptimization.ThumbData

/-!
# Binary search on the machine code

TACLeBench's `binarysearch_binary_search` as Clang 18 compiles it for the Cortex-M0 (`-O1`),
moved to `0x100` (the literal word at the end is the address of the table):

```
low = 0; up = 14; fvalue = -1;
while (low <= up) {
  mid = (low + up) >> 1;
  if (data[mid].key == x) { up = low - 1; fvalue = data[mid].value; }
  else if (data[mid].key > x) up = mid - 1;
  else low = mid + 1;
}
```

The range `up - low + 1` halves at each pass: a logarithmic potential (`LoopCert.log`,
`⌊log₂ Ψ⌋` for an affine `Ψ` of the registers at each instruction). `up = low - 1` wraps around to
`2^32 - 1` when `low = 0`, and so does `up = mid - 1` when `mid = 0`; both join the third
path before the test `low <= up` (signed). There no affine `Ψ` of the registers serves every
state: the invariant has a disjunct for the wrap-around (`alt`), and the potentials differ
between the disjuncts (`LoopCert.potAlt`, `potAtD`: another affine expression where `up` has
wrapped around). The certificate (made by `benchmarks/cycles/binloops.py`, its richer
analysis; the potentials are those its linear program found) bounds the loop at
3 back edges (four passes over 15 entries), with no trusted row; the function takes at most
100 cycles. Without the potentials of the disjuncts the same certificate is refused
(`exBs_needs_alt`).
-/

